			<div class="content" id="post-content-4710761">
									

<p>Viruses can't be 100% lethal, then it doesn't work as a virus. It has to spread. If it kills the host, then that breaks the point of the virus, which is to proliferate. Alongside this, 100% lethality is physically impossible, give your immune system some credit, it's been around the block for literally all of human history, it's learned how to do its job.</p>
<p>Lethal viruses aren't lethal because they want to be or because it's an effective strategy, diseases that are lethal are lethal because they aren't made for humans. They're made for a different animal, a different biology, and so their attempts to hijack your system kill you. Every epidemic, plague, etc. all come from animals: HIV and AIDs(Monkeys, some say Chimps), Swine Flu(pigs), rabies(juries out chief), Avian flu(birbs), Antrax(Grazing animals, sheep, goats, cows, etc.), I could go on, but I'd just be spitting wiki after this.</p>
<p>Why do viruses guarantee immunity(not as you postulate, only the ones we know)? Well, because that how humans develop immunity.<br />
It's not so much that's how viruses work as that's how your immune system works. Each of us builds millions of B Lymphocytes each of whom has hundreds of membrane-bound antibodies each perfectly tailored to an antigen. Viruses will always have some kind of hallmark and should a B Lymphocyte find one that matches, it'll mass copy and go Hunter-Killer on that virus. Oversimplifying and skipping some stuff but yeah.</p>

