																		Chemistry nitpicking
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/seanmichaelragan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1106688); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1106688&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609924117" alt="seanmichaelragan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1106688)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/seanmichaelragan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1106688); return false;" >seanmichaelragan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1312859628 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Aug 2011 03:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1224445">
									

<p>If this prion is of similar size to PrP, the real-world Prion Protein. then &quot;synthesis&quot; is not a danger as implied by the picture caption, unless the SCP universe includes radically superior peptide synthesis technology than the current state of the art. The upper practical limit of completely synthetic peptides is around 30 amino acids, these days, and does not promise to get much larger anytime soon. PrP weighs in at 253 AA.</p>
<p>The gene that codes for SCP-008 might be spliced into a bacterium and &quot;expressed&quot; by raising large cultures of the modified bacteria. The SCP-008 protein might then be extracted from the cultures. But there's more to proteins than just their AA sequences. There's also &quot;post translational modification&quot; which happens at various points along the natural protein biosynthesis process, and even if a culture could be raised that expressed the SCP-008 perfectly on a primary amino acid sequence level, it still might not function as the infectious protein because the culture system might not correctly fold the protein or perform other post-translational modifications.</p>
<p>Anyway, point being: Just displaying the ribbon diagram or even actually publishing the amino acid sequence of SCP-008 really would not present a significant threat since it would be difficult to the point of impossibility to reproduce the infectious material just with that information. As with real-world PrP, the only way to mass-produce SCP-008 would be to get your hands on some that already exists and culture it. Still, you might err on the side of caution and redact the AA sequence anyway. But there really is no danger of &quot;synthesis.&quot;</p>
<p>Suggest caption read: &quot;Ribbon diagram illustrating SCP-008 tertiary structure. Primary amino acid sequence has been redacted.&quot;</p>
<p>Thanks for enduring my pedantry.</p>
<p>Cheers-SMR</p>

