			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1249630">
									

<p>Tineye doesn't recognize it, but it's obviously a protein. Possibly some kind of carrier protein, or maybe a folding chaperone, but I'm only saying that because of the symmetrical cavity at the center. Or maybe it's part of a selective membrane, I don't really know.</p>
<p>That's really all I can say based on this image. We can see the quaternary structure, we can pretty much see the tertiary structure, if you know what to look for you can kind of see the secondary structure, and there's no way to see the primary structure at all &#8212; plus, of course, there's the conformation; no way of knowing how many possible misfolds there are for something this size (the numbers are quite literally more than astronomical), and even a misfold may still be valid.</p>
<p>Finally, we have no indication whatsoever as to its function or origin.</p>
<p>As for whether it reminds anyone else of Christmas - it's primarily green and red, and its shape is evocative of a wreath. So&#8230; no, you must be hallucinating.</p>

